      "text": "We can work with any variable for $x$ as long as it is an integer.\nSince we are also given that $x = 15336 \\cdot k$ for some integer $k$, we get\n\\[f(x) = (3x + 4)(7x + 1)(13x + 6)(2x + 9).\\]Clearly, all of these are multiples of $13, 9, 7,$ and $3$.\nBut we may not use $x = 15336 \\cdot k$ --\nnow consider $f(k)=f(x)$ where $k$ is an integer between $0$ and $15336 - 1.$\nWe have $f(k) = (3k + 4)(7k + 1)(13k + 6)(2k + 9)$ and notice that by the Factor Theorem, this function has roots at $-4/3, -1/7, -6/13$ and $-9/2$.  Now we can hope for divisibility by some of these factors.  If we were to look at each number close to $x$, we would see that $15336 = 3^3 \\cdot 2^5 \\cdot 11$, called the prime factorization.  Adding 1 to $15336$ gives 15337, which is the next largest number, 2 more than the highest power of $2$ in the prime factorization.  Multiplying by the largest remaining factor gives $1$.  This gives us a maximum value of 7, 5, 11, and 200.  We continue and find that $f(7)=144996080$ is a multiple of 200, and 17967 = $(2^3 \\cdot 3^2\\cdot 11 \\cdot 311)$ and 32255 = $(5 \\cdot 1045 \\cdot 311$, so $f(2)=169033120$ is not a multiple of 2^3 or 5.  This shows that $f(200) =14{,}522{,}4644{,}5120 = 2^4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3^3 \\cdot 11^2 \\cdot 17^2 \\cdot 311^2$ is the largest possible value of the function.",
