      "text": "We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor.\nLet $d$ be the greatest common divisor of $f(x)$ and $x$.\nSince $x$ is a multiple of $15336$, it can be written as $x = 15336n$ for some integer $n$.\nThen, $f(x) = (3(15336n) + 4)(7(15336n) + 1)(13(15336n) + 6)(2(15336n) + 9)$.\nSince $15336n$ is a multiple of $15336$, it can be written as $15336m$ for some integer $m$.\nThen, $f(x) = (3(15336m) + 4)(7(15336m) + 1)(13(15336m) + 6)(2(15336m) + 9)$.\nNow, we can break down the terms in the expression to find the greatest common divisor.\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= (3(15336m) + 4)(7(15336m) + 1)(13(15336m) + 6)(2(15336m) + 9) \\\\\n&= (31004(2m) + 4)(92352m + 1)(199088m + 6)(30672m + 9) \\\\\n&= (31004(2m) + 4)(36509m + 1)(199088m + 6)(30672m + 9).\n\\end{align*}\nThe greatest common divisor of these terms will be the greatest common divisor of $31004$, $36509$, $199088$, and $30672$, because we have multiplied each of these constants by the same integer $2m + 1$.\nSince none of these constants have any common prime factors, the greatest common divisor is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
