      "text": "I see two ways to solve this; one counts the total number of factors, and the other sixth powers.\n\nLet $A$, $B$ and $C$ be the factors of 15336. I use $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\gcd(A \\text{ (or B or C)}, f(x))$, if $15336 = A$, $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\gcd(B \\text{ (or C)}, f(x))$, and $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\gcd(A,B \\text{ (or C)}, f(x))$. I pick $x = 1$, $x = \\text{the largest prime factor of 15336}$ and $x = 1 + \\text{the largest prime factor of 15336}$, and find that the gcd is 15336. To explain why $\\text{the largest prime factor of 15336}^{-1} \\cdot 6 x = \\text{the largest prime factor of 29888}$, I perform a quick long division using prime factorization: $6 x = (6 x \\text{ 's first factor}) + (6 x \\text{ 's second factor}) = 2 x \\times 3 \\times 2 x + 2 x \\times 2 x \\times 3 = 27 x + 9 x^2 = 3 . 9 x + 3. 3 x^2$. Thus, the unit digit of $9 \\cdot$ contains the ones digit of 9 $3 x^3$. Therefore, I multiply $29888$'s units digit through by $9$ and add a 4 to find the units digit of $9 \\cdot 29888$. $\\frac{9 \\cdot 29888 + 4}{2} = 9 \\cdot (\\text{units digit of $29888 + 4$} + \\frac{4}{2}) = 9 . 3 + 4. 1 = \\text{part of the units digit of $x$ times 4}$ plus 4. \nSelecting my final units digit by seats 2 factors and 4 factors of 3, my number is divisible by both 12 factor of 12 and 8 of 15336. To find $\\gcd(6 x, f(x))$, I need to find $\\gcd(9 . 3 . 9 . x + 3 . 3 . 3 . x + \\text{(sum of factors that multiply form twice to return to $x$ times the sum of factors that multiply form once}, \\text{ and 4})$, which contains the same factors that $\\gcd(A,B,C, 6 x, f(x)), but do not include any of the 3 factors in $f(x)$ from $x$.\n\nI set $x = 4048$ and perform synthetic division:\n$$\n\\begin{array} {|r|r|rrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrr}|r|r|2008 & {0\\ 1\\ 0\\ 0\\ 0\\ 0\\ 4048}\\\\\\t{(4048)}|{}\\ \\ 2 & {8\\ 1\\ 8\\ 36\\ 238\\ 4736}|\\ \\ 8 \n\\end{array}\n$$\nSince $217=7 \\times 31$, the remainder is 2. Thus, $x$ divides $24040$. Since 24040 evenly divides 29888 and it has 15336 as a factor, 29888 and 24040 do not share common factors other then 24040's 2 and 4. So, I count 6 as another common factor. Thus $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\boxed{15336}$.  # The concept learned here is that $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\gcd(A, f(x))$ if $x = A$, $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\gcd(B, f(x))$ if $x = B$, and if $x = C$, then $\\gcd(15336, f(x)) = \\gcd( A, B, f(x))$\nSecond way: I would be able to prove that $\\gcd(15336, f(x))$ will be equivalent to $\\gcd(3, 7, 13, 2) \\cdot \\%29888 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\%2$. I would then count the divisor of these numbers - 15336, 5664, 1408, 352, 184, 92, 46, 23, 11, 5, 2, and 1. Then, I would have to use analysis - i.e. assume $5664 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2222$, and continue subtracting by 2,3,11,23,7,2, until I reach $1$. At that time, i would multiply by the prime number which is a factor of 5664, 5664/2, 5664/3, 5664/11 etc.\nEventually I'd reach $1$, but that means the product of all the primes--2,3,11 etc is equal to $\\gcd(15336, f(x))",
